sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0243/0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4, single-swamp-node, agent-observer-swamp-acts, root-of-trust-gateway-only, tool.manager_converge removed (shared Tool Manager setup step). docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#add-a-node.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: node:add
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane HUB icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as New Node 12<br/>reached over ssh
  participant HUB as WireGuard hub<br/>vpn Node 1
  CLI->>GW: node:add beast 203.0.113.7<br/>--host-key-fingerprint=… --role=…
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit node:add beast 203.0.113.7 --host-key-fingerprint=SHA256:… --role=app-dev --tld=test`
  %%> - syntax: `<name> [host] [--host-key-fingerprint=SHA256] [--ssh-port=PORT] [--user=USER] [--orbit-user=USER] [--platform=linux|macos] [--architecture=ARCH] [--tld=TLD] [--role=ROLE]… [--cluster=ID] [--wireguard-ip=IP] [--lan-ip=IP] [--wireguard-endpoint=HOST:PORT] [--dns-server=IP] [--setting=PATH:VALUE]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Node name and its public SSH host
  %%> - the approved SSH host key fingerprint; required for a new Node
  %%> - optionally roles, a TLD, a Cluster and network overrides
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - adds the machine to the fleet as Node 12, named `beast`
  %%> - for a Node that already exists, the same command converges it again with its new TLD, roles or settings
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate, record Node 12<br/>provisioning · task #6201
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the name is free, or names a Node that owns no Instances (`node.has_instances`)
  %%> - a new Node has a host and a host key fingerprint (`node.ssh_host_fingerprint_required`)
  %%> - Linux accepts only `x86_64`; `app-dev` needs a TLD, unless its Cluster has one
  %%> - macOS names one existing account in `--user` and `--orbit-user`, an existing WireGuard address, and no role
  %%> - no other add, converge or removal runs for this Node; the Gateway holds one lock per Node and one for the hub
  %%> Builds:
  %%> - the WireGuard address `10.6.0.42` and the hub peer entry
  %%> - the bootstrap script, base packages, apt Tool Manager setup and public SSH rule
  %%> - the Orbit SSH block, the pinned `orbit-agent` release and the Node agent configuration
  %%> - the role steps for each requested role
  %%> Result:
  %%> - Node 12 recorded as `provisioning`; an existing Node keeps its record until the result
  %%> - task #6201 recorded with the full intent
  %%> - nothing has happened on the machine yet
  GW->>SW: Push keys to vault<br/>bootstrap-identity-node-12 · agent
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the first login uses the bootstrap SSH identity, a private key that only the Gateway holds
  %%> - the Node agent needs a new secret, and secrets travel only through the vault (R3)
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - pushes Node 12's bootstrap identity as vault key `bootstrap-identity-node-12`
  %%> - pushes Node 12's new Node agent secret as vault key `agent-secret-node-12`
  %%> Never:
  %%> - a fixed key name for every Node, or a key value in the run inputs
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the run reads both keys from the vault; no step calls the Gateway back
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-add
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the host, port, bootstrap user, managed user and approved fingerprint
  %%> - the bootstrap script, packages, pins, Node agent configuration and role steps
  %%> - the hub's address and SSH host keys, and the new peer entry
  %%> - secrets only as vault key names
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #6201
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  SW->>N: Probe host key and arch<br/>@swamp/ssh · pinned fingerprint
  %%> How:
  %%> - Swamp reaches 203.0.113.7 over `@swamp/ssh` from 209 with the bootstrap identity from the vault
  %%> - refuses to run a command when the host key differs from the approved fingerprint (`node.ssh_host_key_mismatch`)
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - reads the platform and architecture with `uname -sm`; `amd64` counts as `x86_64`
  %%> - a Linux machine that is not `x86_64` fails with `node.architecture_unsupported`
  %%> Returns:
  %%> - the host key, platform and architecture as step outputs
  alt Linux Node
    SW->>N: Bootstrap the host<br/>packages · user · apt Tool Manager
    %%> Runs as:
    %%> - the bootstrap user: `root` directly for a new Node, the managed user through passwordless sudo for an existing one
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - installs the base packages; a Node without roles keeps its own DNS
    %%> - creates the managed user `orbit` when it is missing and checks passwordless sudo before it publishes the rule
    %%> - sets up the apt Tool Manager with the shared Tool Manager setup step
    %%> - adds the UFW rule `orbit:public-ssh-recovery` and enables UFW
    %%> How:
    %%> - official `@swamp/cfgmgmt` behaviors (apt, file) and one tracked script over `@swamp/ssh`
    %%> Replaces:
    %%> - the `tool.manager_converge` child; the setup is a step here
    SW->>N: Install the Node agent<br/>pinned orbit-agent · sha256
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - downloads the pinned `orbit-agent` release and checks its SHA-256
    %%> - a matching installed binary skips the download
    %%> - publishes the Orbit CLI on an eligible Node, best effort
    %%> How:
    %%> - official `@swamp/cfgmgmt` behaviors (fetch, file) over SSH from 209
    %%> Not included:
    %%> - starts nothing; the agent starts after enrollment
    SW->>N: Set up WireGuard<br/>key · orbit.conf · wg-quick@orbit
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - creates the WireGuard key on Node 12; an existing key stays
    %%> - writes `/etc/wireguard/orbit.conf` for `10.6.0.42` with its DNS policy, and starts `wg-quick@orbit`
    %%> - adds the UFW rule `orbit:wireguard-members`; public SSH stays open
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the public key as a step output; the private key never leaves the Node
    SW->>HUB: Add the peer<br/>wg syncconf · no restart
    %%> How:
    %%> - Swamp reaches the `vpn` Node over `@swamp/ssh` from 209; there is no worker on Node 1
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - adds Node 12's peer with the public key from the step before
    %%> - validates the new hub configuration with `wg-quick strip`, keeps the live file as a backup and swaps it in
    %%> - live-syncs the running hub with `wg syncconf`; other peers keep their tunnels
    SW->>N: Enroll the Node agent<br/>vault.get agent-secret-node-12
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - writes `config.toml`, `ca.pem`, the secret and the `orbit-agent.service` unit in `/etc/orbit/agent`
    %%> - starts the Node agent; it reports to the Gateway over WireGuard
    %%> Observer only:
    %%> - the Node agent observes and reports; it runs no actions
    %%> How:
    %%> - official `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file` and `@swamp/cfgmgmt/systemd` behaviors over SSH from 209
    SW->>N: Write the Orbit SSH block<br/>authorized_keys · marked block
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - writes the `swamp` role's public key and the recorded operator keys for the managed user, between the Orbit markers
    %%> - the same step as `node:ssh-access:converge`; keys outside the block stay as they are
    %%> Why:
    %%> - every later command reaches Node 12 as the managed user with the `swamp` role identity, not the bootstrap identity
    opt roles requested
      SW->>N: Converge each role<br/>shared role converge steps
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - runs the shared role steps for each requested role over WireGuard, `vpn` first when it is requested
      %%> - the first active role closes public SSH
      %%> Same as:
      %%> - `node:role:add`; there is no child task per role
    end
    SW->>HUB: Publish private DNS<br/>beast records · reload dnsmasq
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - adds Node 12's private names to the DNS catalog on the `vpn` Node
    %%> - reloads the DNS listener only when the records changed; the last good copy comes back when the new one fails its check
    %%> Cluster:
    %%> - a Node in a Cluster also gets the Router's LAN rules, through the shared firewall step
  else macOS Node
    SW->>N: Inspect the Mac<br/>sw_vers · uname -m · tunnel addr
    %%> Runs as:
    %%> - the existing account; no user, sudo rule, package, firewall or DNS change
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - reads the platform, the architecture and the WireGuard address with plain read commands
    %%> - checks them against the request; a difference fails with `node.platform_mismatch` or `node.architecture_mismatch`
    %%> Not included:
    %%> - no Node agent, apt, roles, hub change or Orbit SSH block; the Mac already has its tunnel
    %%> Replaces:
    %%> - the `node.macos_inspect` child
  end
  SW->>N: Check the Node<br/>ssh over WireGuard · agent · arch
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - logs in over WireGuard (`10.6.0.42`) as the managed user, with the `swamp` role identity only
  %%> - checks the architecture again, and on Linux that `orbit-agent` is active and runs the pinned binary
  %%> - checks the WireGuard handshake with the hub
  %%> Why:
  %%> - Node 12 only counts as active when Swamp reaches it the way every later command does
  alt every step passes
    N-->>SW: arch · host key · WG key
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the architecture, the host key and the WireGuard public key
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the run's outputs; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record Node 12 active<br/>pins · roles · task succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Node 12 becomes active, with its architecture, SSH host key pins and WireGuard key
    %%> - each converged role becomes active; storage settings are stored
    %%> - task #6201 succeeds
    %%> Next:
    %%> - Metrics exporters start only with `metrics:exporter:enable`
  else a step fails
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or mismatch
    %%> When:
    %%> - the host key or architecture differs, the bootstrap or a role fails, the hub refuses its configuration, or the check fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code
    Note over GW: Mark Node 12 failed<br/>task failed · rerun is safe
    %%> Result:
    %%> - a new Node 12 stays, marked failed at that step, so you can see it
    %%> - an existing Node keeps its earlier record and stays active
    %%> - task #6201 fails
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback
    %%> - the hub peer stays; it is harmless and a rerun reuses it
    %%> - a failed hub swap puts its backup back, so other peers keep working
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same `node:add` again after fixing the cause; every step skips work that is already done
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #6201
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
